Peripersonal space (PPS) is often inferred from tactile reaction time during an approaching sound. We tested whether instructed respiratory phase changed this SOA-dependent response differently in breath-coupled virtual reality (Viscereality) and Control. The released cohort comprised 42 participant records and 8,916 retained audio--tactile hit trials. The planned Condition $times$ Phase $times$ SOA mixed-model interaction was not detected, $chi^2 \( 1 \) = 0.024$, $p = .878$; categorical-SOA and partial-session-exclusion sensitivities agreed. Audio--tactile responses were 18.8~ms faster than tactile-only Baseline responses on average, but equal nominal delay labels do not establish matched cue times. None of 168 participant-by-condition-by-phase trial-level sigmoid fits met the planned $R^2 gt.eq .70$ threshold. The maximum saturated five-SOA trial-level $R^2$ ceiling was 0.430, precluding this threshold for any curve of SOA alone. Participant-boundary and boundary-related questionnaire inference therefore remained unestimated. A separate post hoc comparison of 800 uniformly processed sigmoid and linear pipelines produced four explicit shape-constrained approximations. Their visible S shapes were imposed; nested participant prediction did not improve on a constant, and unsmoothed curvature tests did not establish nonlinearity. Midpoint and steepness estimates remain assumption-dependent descriptions. An exploratory paired ordinal comparison in 41 participants found higher post-intervention spatial-frame codes under Viscereality (exact sign-test $p = 0.0037$). Pre-experiment MAIA-2 Trusting correlated with the numerical Viscereality--Control difference ($rho = 0.376$, unadjusted $p = 0.0160$); its moderation estimate remained positive with order and HC3 sensitivities. These post hoc associations are not evidence of mediation. A further exploratory extension produced 168 individual sigmoid proxies and common-plateau displays, but midpoint reliability was low and all main refit-bootstrap association intervals included zero. An audit found two identical behavioural recordings with reversed intervention labels and different questionnaire responses; full-release behavioural inference is provisional pending source mapping. The full data-supported pipeline, diagnostics, and downloadable results run in a marimo browser companion. These data do not demonstrate the predicted Viscereality-specific respiratory modulation; confidence intervals and source-timing and respiratory-compliance limits constrain interpretation.
